      "content": "# dsh-plugin-rea — one-plugin bundle\n#\n# Registers the REA MCP server as mcp__rea__* and launches it through the\n# bundle's own shim, which reads the machine-local state written by\n# `scripts/setup.mjs` and exports exactly the variables REA reads.\n#\n# PORTABLE BY CONSTRUCTION — no machine path appears in this file.\n#   * `command` is the running Node executable (`process.execPath`), so the\n#     launcher needs no PATH entry and no shim resolution by the OS.\n#   * `args[0]` is computed at boot from DSH_HOME, where setup mirrors the shim.\n#   * FORWARD SLASHES ONLY in the JS string literals below. A single-quoted YAML\n#     scalar keeps backslashes literal, and JavaScript then eats them: '\\plugins'\n#     is not \"\\plugins\" but the BEL control character, '\\rea-bundle' becomes a\n#     carriage return and '\\bin' a backspace, so the path is silently destroyed.\n#     Forward slashes are accepted on Windows and are correct on POSIX, so one\n#     expression serves every platform.\n#   * `path`/`require` are not in scope for a `!!js` expression (`ctx` is the\n#     loader scope), hence plain string concatenation.\n#   * No DSH_HOME (undefined/absent/empty) falls back to the SAME rule the shim\n#     and setup.mjs use — `<home>/.dsh` — never to the bare home directory.\n#     Getting this wrong is silent: setup would mirror the shim into\n#     `<home>/.dsh/plugins/rea-bundle/bin/` while the row looked for it one level\n#     up, so the row could never start on a machine that merely happened to have\n#     DSH_HOME unset.\n#   * The home fallback mirrors Node's own `os.homedir()`: USERPROFILE, then\n#     HOMEDRIVE+HOMEPATH (the shape a service account gets), then HOME. An\n#     expression that checked only USERPROFILE and HOME produced `/.dsh/...` —\n#     the root of the current drive — for a service account, which is neither\n#     where setup mirrors the shim nor a path anything can read.\n#\n# One row may use serverName: rea in a profile. To pin a different launch\n# command on this machine, override `mcp-rea` from the profile's own\n# cordis.patch.yml (id-targeted config override) — that file is never published.\n\n- insert:\n    - id: mcp-rea\n      name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-client'\n      config:\n        serverName: rea\n        transport: stdio\n        command: !!js process.execPath\n        args:\n          # DOUBLE-QUOTED on purpose. A bare scalar containing \"? \" and \": \" is not\n          # one scalar at all: YAML reads `? a : b` as an explicit key/value pair,\n          # so js-yaml hands the loader a mapping instead of an expression node and\n          # args[0] becomes an object the launcher cannot use. Quoting makes the\n          # scalar unambiguous; the JS string literals inside use single quotes so\n          # they cannot collide with the YAML ones.\n          - !!js \"(process.env.DSH_HOME && process.env.DSH_HOME.trim() !== '' ? process.env.DSH_HOME : (process.env.USERPROFILE || ((process.env.HOMEDRIVE || '') + (process.env.HOMEPATH || '')) || process.env.HOME || '') + '/.dsh') + '/plugins/rea-bundle/bin/rea-shim.mjs'\"\n          - mcp\n        env:\n          # Only meaningful when the harness itself runs on Electron; harmless\n          # for a plain Node host. Explicit is better than relying on this\n          # variable happening to survive the child-env scrub.\n          ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE: '1'\n        failOnStartupError: false\n\n    # The Web GUI's one-shot setup guide. This row loads only the package entry\n    # (index.mjs), which serves a read-only GET /dsh-plugin-rea/guide-state and\n    # mounts nothing when the host has no webServer. It does not launch REA, does\n    # not provision, and never runs setup for the user — the guide only reports\n    # that the recorded bundle does not match the pin this package ships.\n    - id: rea-guide\n      name: 'dsh-plugin-rea'\n"
